 "facts": [
  {
   "claim": "View of Scheveningen Sands by Hendrick van Anthonissen (1605–1656), painted around 1641; shows groups of people on the beach at Scheveningen and others on the dunes/cliffs; Scheveningen is a seaside district of The Hague",
   "source": "https://www.cam.ac.uk/research/news/whale-tale-a-dutch-seascape-and-its-lost-leviathan ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/View_of_Scheveningen_Sands ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scheveningen"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Given to the Fitzwilliam Museum in 1873 by Richard Kerrich with other Dutch landscapes; the documentation shows nobody knew it included a whale",
   "source": "https://www.cam.ac.uk/research/news/whale-tale-a-dutch-seascape-and-its-lost-leviathan ; https://www.thehistoryblog.com/archives/30801"
  },
  {
   "claim": "In 2014 Shan Kuang, a postgraduate conservator at the Hamilton Kerr Institute, removed the old varnish and overpaint",
   "source": "https://www.cam.ac.uk/research/news/whale-tale-a-dutch-seascape-and-its-lost-leviathan ; https://www.varsity.co.uk/news/7300"
  },
  {
   "claim": "While cleaning, a small figure appeared standing on the horizon, next to 'a shape that looked like a sail'; the 'sail' was the whale's fin, and the man was standing on the whale's back",
   "source": "https://www.cam.ac.uk/research/news/whale-tale-a-dutch-seascape-and-its-lost-leviathan ; https://www.varsity.co.uk/news/7300"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Under a thick layer of repaint lay a beached (stranded) sperm whale, the original focus of the composition",
   "source": "https://www.cam.ac.uk/research/news/whale-tale-a-dutch-seascape-and-its-lost-leviathan ; https://www.varsity.co.uk/news/7300 ; https://www.thehistoryblog.com/archives/30801"
  },
  {
   "claim": "The overpaint was not by the artist; analysis dates it to the 18th or 19th century, before 1873; a previous owner probably had it painted out (hedged: 'experts think')",
   "source": "https://www.cam.ac.uk/research/news/whale-tale-a-dutch-seascape-and-its-lost-leviathan ; https://www.varsity.co.uk/news/7300 ; https://www.thehistoryblog.com/archives/30801"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Why is unknown: Kuang suggested a dead animal may have been considered offensive, or the picture was more marketable without the whale",
   "source": "https://www.cam.ac.uk/research/news/whale-tale-a-dutch-seascape-and-its-lost-leviathan ; https://www.varsity.co.uk/news/7300"
  },
  {
   "claim": "The restored painting, whale included, is on permanent display at the Fitzwilliam Museum, Cambridge (Dutch Golden Age gallery)",
   "source": "https://www.varsity.co.uk/news/7300 ; https://www.cam.ac.uk/research/news/whale-tale-a-dutch-seascape-and-its-lost-leviathan"
  },
  {
   "claim": "On-screen print: beached sperm whale at Beverwijk (stranded 1601), engraving by Jan Saenredam, 1602",
   "source": "https://www.thehistoryblog.com/archives/30801 ; https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gestrande_walvis_bij_Beverwijk,_1601,_RP-P-OB-4635.jpg"
  }
 ],
 "caption": "Around 1641, Hendrick van Anthonissen painted the beach at Scheveningen, near The Hague: crowds on the sand, people on the dunes, all looking out to sea. At nothing, it seemed.\n\nIn 1873 the painting was given to the Fitzwilliam Museum in Cambridge. Nobody knew anything was missing. Then in 2014 student conservator Shan Kuang, at the Hamilton Kerr Institute, began removing the old varnish. A tiny man appeared, standing on the horizon, next to what looked like a sail.\n\nIt was a fin. Under a thick layer of later paint lay a stranded sperm whale, with the man standing on its back. Experts think a later owner had it painted out, in the 18th or 19th century. Maybe a dead animal seemed offensive. Maybe the picture simply sold better.\n\nP.S. The museum notes there were many whale strandings on the Dutch coast in the first half of the 17th century.\n\n🐋 Would you have bought it with the whale?",
 "caption_short": "Someone painted a dead whale out of this 1641 beach scene. In 2014, a conservator found it again. 🐋",
 "youtube_title": "Someone painted out the whale | van Anthonissen, Scheveningen Sands",
